---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import HelpModal from '../components/HelpModal.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const title = 'About Online Ruler Plus | Precision Screen Metrology';
const description =
  'Learn how Online Ruler Plus turns screens into calibrated physical measuring rulers built on NIST, ISO/IEC 7810, and W3C CSS standards.';
const canonicalUrl = 'https://onlinerulerplus.com/about';

const structuredData = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  '@id': `${canonicalUrl}#about`,
  name: 'About Online Ruler Plus',
  description,
  url: canonicalUrl,
  inLanguage: 'en',
  mainEntity: {
    '@id': 'https://onlinerulerplus.com/#organization',
  },
  speakable: {
    '@type': 'SpeakableSpecification',
    cssSelector: ['h1', '.speakable-summary'],
  },
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/about"
  breadcrumbLabel="About"
  structuredData={structuredData}
>
  <Header subNavTitle="About" subNavCtaHref="/#ruler-tool" subNavCtaLabel="Open Ruler" />
  <main id="main-content" class="flex-1">
    <section class="apple-page-hero border-b border-hairline">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-[1440px] px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-6 lg:flex-row lg:items-end lg:justify-between">
          <div class="max-w-3xl">
            <p class="type-kicker">About the Platform · Metrology Methodology &amp; Publisher Accountability</p>
            <h1 class="mt-2 type-hero-display text-ink">
              About Online Ruler Plus
            </h1>
            <p class="speakable-summary mt-3 max-w-[62ch] type-lead-airy text-ink-muted-80">
              Online Ruler Plus is an independent, client-side screen metrology web application engineered to turn any desktop, laptop, tablet, or smartphone display into a calibrated physical measuring instrument with zero camera prompts.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="shrink-0">
            <a href="/#ruler-tool" class="apple-btn-primary">
              Return to Online Ruler
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="apple-tile-parchment">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-[1440px] px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 lg:grid-cols-3">
          <article class="apple-utility-card flex flex-col justify-between">
            <div>
              <p class="type-kicker">01 · Why We Built It</p>
              <h2 class="mt-2 type-tagline text-ink">What is the 96 CSS PPI fallacy in online rulers?</h2>
              <p class="mt-2.5 max-w-[65ch] type-body text-ink-muted-80">
                The 96 CSS PPI fallacy is the widespread engineering mistake of assuming that 96 browser CSS pixels equal 1.00 physical inch on every user’s screen. Under the W3C CSS Values and Units Module Level 4 specification, 1 CSS inch is permanently locked to 96 CSS pixels (3.7795 CSS pixels per millimeter) so typography scales legibly at viewing distance. However, consumer hardware panels range from 81.6 CSS PPI on 27-inch desktop monitors to over 150 CSS PPI on smartphones and Retina laptops, causing uncalibrated web rulers to err by 10% to 35%. Online Ruler Plus was built to solve this flaw by placing ISO/IEC 7810 physical calibration at the core of the rendering engine.
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>

          <article class="apple-utility-card flex flex-col justify-between">
            <div>
              <p class="type-kicker">02 · Metrology Standards</p>
              <h2 class="mt-2 type-tagline text-ink">Which international standards govern our calculations?</h2>
              <p class="mt-2.5 max-w-[65ch] type-body text-ink-muted-80">
                Online Ruler Plus derives every tick coordinate and conversion readout from published international standards rather than heuristic approximations. First, metric-to-imperial conversion follows the 1959 international yard and pound agreement maintained by NIST and the BIPM, where 1 inch equals 25.4 millimeters exactly and 1 centimeter equals 10 millimeters. Second, physical card calibration conforms to ISO/IEC 7810:2019 ID-1 (85.60 × 53.98 mm, corner radius 3.18 mm). Third, secondary coin verification benchmarks follow United States Mint (US Quarter: 24.26 mm, US Penny: 19.05 mm) and European Central Bank (1 Euro: 23.25 mm) specifications.
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>

          <article class="apple-utility-card flex flex-col justify-between">
            <div>
              <p class="type-kicker">03 · Engineering Principles</p>
              <h2 class="mt-2 type-tagline text-ink">How does Online Ruler Plus protect accuracy and user focus?</h2>
              <p class="mt-2.5 max-w-[65ch] type-body text-ink-muted-80">
                Our architecture enforces three strict engineering invariants across every ruler page. First, mathematical honesty: status badges explicitly distinguish between uncalibrated 96 PPI estimates, detected hardware profiles, and user-verified manual calibrations—never claiming physical certainty from browser detection alone. Second, local measurement execution: all calibration profiles (<code class="font-mono text-ink">orp:calibration:v1</code>) and measurement history (<code class="font-mono text-ink">orp:history:v1</code>) remain inside your browser’s <code class="font-mono text-ink">localStorage</code> with zero camera prompts or measurement uploads. Third, high-contrast HTML5 canvas rendering scaled by <code class="font-mono text-ink">window.devicePixelRatio</code> ensures sharp 1 mm and 1/16″ graduations.
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="apple-tile-light border-t border-hairline">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-[1440px] px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
          <article class="apple-utility-card">
            <p class="type-kicker">04 · Publisher Accountability &amp; Verification Policy</p>
            <h2 class="mt-2 type-tagline text-ink">Who publishes OnlineRulerPlus.com and how are guides verified?</h2>
            <p class="mt-2.5 max-w-[65ch] type-body text-ink-muted-80">
              OnlineRulerPlus.com is published and maintained by the Online Ruler Plus engineering project as an open, client-side web utility. Rather than publishing unverified blog claims or copied reference tables, every mathematical constant, fractional millimeter conversion, and screen-density example in our guides is derived directly from primary published standards (NIST Special Publication data, ISO/IEC 7810 ID-1, W3C CSS Values Level 4, and ISO 216 paper dimensions) and validated through automated unit and browser tests prior to deployment.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="apple-utility-card">
            <p class="type-kicker">05 · Corrections, Accessibility &amp; Direct Support</p>
            <h2 class="mt-2 type-tagline text-ink">How can you report an error, request a fix, or submit display feedback?</h2>
            <p class="mt-2.5 max-w-[65ch] type-body text-ink-muted-80">
              Accuracy documentation and WCAG 2.2 AA accessibility are treated as ongoing commitments. Whenever operating system display scaling behavior changes or users report a hardware mismatch, we verify the reproduction case and update the corresponding guide or display profile with transparent review timestamps. If you spot a typographical error, keyboard navigation gap, or display density discrepancy, please reach our engineering team at <a href="mailto:support@onlinerulerplus.com" class="text-link underline">support@onlinerulerplus.com</a> or follow the steps on our <a href="/contact" class="text-link underline">Contact &amp; Submissions page</a>.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <HelpModal />
  <Footer />
  <script src="../scripts/app.ts"></script>
</BaseLayout>
